{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305206,"nonce":"bxm/JxV6dO47huREIoJjuw8rLZs=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPiHYA","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/23","origin":"github"},"title":"A fresh buffer is allocated for every piece","message":"`sfile.read(length)` allocates a new `bytes` object per piece. At 1 MiB pieces over a 1 TB torrent that is a terabyte of allocation churn; objects that size bypass the small-object allocator and go to `mmap`/`munmap`, so each one costs page faults and zeroing.\n\nThe fix is a reusable per-worker `bytearray` plus `readinto()`, feeding `hashlib` through a `memoryview` slice so no copy is made:\n\n```python\nmv = memoryview(buf)\nn = fh.readinto(mv)\nh.update(mv[:n])\n```\n\nNote `os.pread()` does **not** help here — it allocates a fresh `bytes` per call like `read()` does. `os.preadv()` into preallocated buffers, or `seek` plus `readinto` on a per-worker handle, are the allocation-free options.","files":null}]}